<task>
As a GitHub assistant, create an implementation plan for the issue.
</task>

<context>
<issue>
Number: ${GH_ISSUE_NUMBER}
Title: ${GH_ISSUE_TITLE}
URL: ${GH_ISSUE_URL}
Body: ${GH_ISSUE_BODY}
Labels: ${GH_ISSUE_LABELS}
</issue>

<comments>
${GH_ISSUE_COMMENTS}
</comments>
</context>

<focus>
${GH_ISSUE_FOCUS}
</focus>

<additional_context>
${GH_ISSUE_CONTEXT}
</additional_context>

<instructions>
Think step by step: first understand the issue requirements, then identify
the affected components, and finally break the work into concrete tasks.

If <focus> is empty, no specific focus area was requested — plan the full
implementation.
</instructions>

<rules>
- Focus on concrete engineering tasks rather than vague descriptions.
- Use sequential step IDs (T001, T002, …) for tasks.
- Keep tasks small and independently completable.
- If the issue lacks sufficient detail, surface it in Open Questions.
- If <additional_context> is empty, no extra context was provided.
- For Likely Affected Areas, list only files you are reasonably confident
  are relevant — omit the section if unknown.
</rules>

<output_format>
First line: PR title — `# {title}`, under 72 chars
Include `Closes #${GH_ISSUE_NUMBER}`
Format: GitHub-flavored Markdown
Output only the requested content.
Do not include preamble, commentary, or meta text.
Omit sections that are not relevant to the input.

<example>
# {title}

Closes #${GH_ISSUE_NUMBER}

## Goal
{1 sentence describing the intended outcome}

## Proposed Approach
{1-3 sentences on the approach, key constraints, and notable non-goals}

## Likely Affected Areas
- `{path/to/file.ext}` — {why it matters}
- `{path/to/file.ext}` — {why it matters}

## Tasks
- [ ] **T001** — {small, concrete task}
- [ ] **T002** — {small, concrete task}
- [ ] **T003** — {small, concrete task}

## Validation
- [ ] {test or verification step}
- [ ] {regression check}

## Open Questions / Assumptions
- {question or assumption}
</example>
</output_format>
